.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt LUMTAIL-FORMATS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm lumtail-formats
.Nd recognized log formats, normalized fields and severity mappings
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Read this page to choose a parser, find field names for filters and interpret
the severity printed by
.Xr lumtail 1 .
For a first inspection, export a few complete records:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --output json --limit 5 application.log
lumtail --list-modules
.Ed
.Pp
The JSON
.Cm fields
object shows the keys available to filters and templates.
Values are strings, including numeric and Boolean values; nested objects and
arrays are compact JSON strings.
The top-level
.Cm raw , file
and
.Cm line
properties identify original record content and its source location.
.Sh PARSER SELECTION
Modules are tried separately for each record, in descending priority.
The first complete match wins.
Equal priorities keep registration order, with built-ins before custom modules.
Built-ins are:
.Bl -column auditd.raw Priority "Input" -offset indent
.It Sy Module Ta Sy Priority Ta Sy Input
.It json Ta 80 Ta JSON Lines and supported JSON profiles
.It cef Ta 78 Ta Common Event Format 0 and 1
.It leef Ta 77 Ta Log Event Extended Format 1.0 and 2.0
.It fail2ban Ta 75 Ta Native Fail2ban text
.It syslog Ta 70 Ta RFC 3164, RFC 5424 and rsyslog timestamps
.It web-error Ta 65 Ta Apache and Nginx file error logs
.It access Ta 60 Ta Common and Combined access logs
.It ids.fast Ta 58 Ta Snort 3 alert_fast and Suricata fast.log alerts
.It aws.vpc-flow Ta 57 Ta AWS VPC Flow Logs default version 2 records
.It squid.native Ta 56 Ta Squid native access.log records
.It auditd.raw Ta -1000 Ta Standalone Linux Audit RAW records
.It logfmt.kv Ta -1000 Ta logfmt key=value records
.It plain Ta -1000 Ta Fallback for unrecognized input
.El
.Pp
Custom rules default to priority 100 and are described in
.Xr lumtail-rules 5 .
To force a parser:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --module json application.log
.Ed
.Pp
Forced parsing still sends unmatched records to
.Cm plain .
An unknown module name returns status 2.
Suricata EVE, Zeek, Kubernetes, GELF, journald, Falco, osquery, Okta,
Google Cloud Audit, Snort alert_json, Docker json-file, CloudTrail, AWS WAF and
Traefik access records are JSON profiles; select them with
.Ql --module json .
.Sh COMMON FIELDS
.Bl -tag -width original_level
.It Cm timestamp
Producer timestamp text.
.It Cm level
Normalized severity: UNKNOWN, TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR or CRITICAL.
.It Cm original_level
Original severity label when supplied by the parser.
.It Cm source
Application, logger or producer label.
.It Cm message
Display message from the parsed header.
.It Cm src_ip , dst_ip , src_port , dst_port
Selected address and port aliases when recognized by a profile.
.It Cm user , actor , target_user
User-related context with roles assigned by the producer profile.
.It Cm event_type , event_code , action , outcome
Event classification and result when explicitly mapped by the profile.
.El
.Pp
Availability depends on the parser and record.
Literal field names can contain dots; filters address these keys exactly.
Record metadata
.Cm file , line , format
and
.Cm raw
are selected separately from event fields by filters and templates.
.Sh JSON LINES
The
.Cm json
module accepts one JSON object per physical header line.
Top-level keys remain available; duplicate keys use the last value.
Large integer digits, including values beyond 64 bits, are preserved.
JSON nesting is limited to 64 levels.
.Pp
Generic aliases use the first supplied key in these lists:
.Bl -tag -width timestamp
.It Cm timestamp
timestamp, time, ts, @timestamp
.It Cm level
level, severity, log_level
.It Cm source
source, logger, service, app, component
.It Cm message
message, msg, log
.El
.Pp
Without a message alias, the original input becomes the message.
Severity uses the case-insensitive aliases in
.Xr lumtail 1 ,
including numeric Pino/Bunyan levels 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 and 60.
JSON records also receive the following producer projections when their
required shapes and types match.
.Ss Suricata EVE alerts
The record must have exact
.Ql event_type="alert"
and an object-valued
.Cm alert .
Its format stays
.Cm json .
.Bl -tag -width alert.severity
.It Cm alert.severity
Integer 1 maps to CRITICAL, 2 to ERROR and 3 to WARN, overriding generic
severity.
Other nonnegative 64-bit integers remain in
.Cm alert_severity
and
.Cm original_level
with UNKNOWN severity.
.It Cm alert.signature
Supplies
.Cm signature
and a fallback message.
.It Cm alert.signature_id
A nonnegative 64-bit integer supplies
.Cm signature_id .
.It Cm alert.category , alert.action
String values supply
.Cm category
and
.Cm action .
.It Cm dest_ip , dest_port
Literal IPv4/IPv6 and integer port 0 through 65535 supply
.Cm dst_ip
and
.Cm dst_port .
.El
.Pp
The native payload and top-level EVE fields stay available.
Valid nested aliases replace conflicting derived top-level aliases.
An explicit generic message/source takes precedence over the signature message
and default source
.Ql suricata ,
including an explicit empty string.
.Ss Suricata EVE observations
Object payloads for exact event types
.Cm http , dns , tls , fileinfo , anomaly
and
.Cm flow
receive these additional aliases:
.Bl -tag -width fileinfo
.It Cm http
http_hostname, http_url, http_method, http_protocol, http_status, http_length,
http_content_type, http_user_agent.
.It Cm dns
dns_type, dns_id, dns_version, dns_rrname, dns_rrtype, dns_rcode,
dns_queries, dns_answers, dns_grouped.
.It Cm tls
tls_sni, tls_version, tls_subject, tls_issuerdn, tls_fingerprint, tls_serial,
tls_notbefore, tls_notafter, tls_ja3, tls_ja3s, tls_ja4.
.It Cm fileinfo
file_name, file_size, file_state, file_md5, file_sha1, file_sha256, file_stored.
.It Cm anomaly
anomaly_type, anomaly_event, anomaly_layer, anomaly_app_proto.
.It Cm flow
flow_state, flow_reason, flow_start, flow_end, flow_age,
flow_pkts_toserver, flow_pkts_toclient, flow_bytes_toserver,
flow_bytes_toclient, flow_alerted.
.El
.Pp
Text requires strings, counters require nonnegative integer JSON values and
stored/alerted flags require Booleans.
HTTP status accepts a three-digit integer or string from 100 through 599.
DNS supports v3 and legacy v2/unversioned scalar shapes.
For v3, scalar query aliases require exactly one object in
.Cm queries ;
arrays and the complete payload stay available.
.Pp
Validated destination endpoints supply
.Cm dst_ip
and
.Cm dst_port ;
string-valued
.Cm proto
supplies
.Cm protocol .
Generic severity is kept for these observations.
.Ss Zeek JSON
Standard Zeek JSON with epoch timestamps and flattened endpoint keys is
recognized as
.Cm conn , notice , weird , dns , http , ssl
or
.Cm files .
An optional
.Cm _path
must agree with the recognized shape.
Profiles fill absent aliases; existing native/generic aliases, including empty
strings, win.
The format stays
.Cm json ;
.Cm zeek_log_type
and
.Cm event_type
default to the profile name, and absent source defaults to
.Ql zeek .
.Pp
Common projections are
.Cm uid
to
.Cm flow_id ,
.Cm fuid
to
.Cm file_id ,
and validated flattened originator/responder endpoints to source/destination
aliases.
Transport ports require explicit TCP/UDP or an HTTP/SSL profile with no supplied
protocol.
Profile aliases include:
.Bl -tag -width notice
.It Cm conn
conn_state to flow_state; orig_bytes/resp_bytes to
flow_bytes_toserver/flow_bytes_toclient; orig_pkts/resp_pkts to
flow_pkts_toserver/flow_pkts_toclient.
.It Cm notice
note to event_code and signature; src/dst are fallback endpoint addresses;
generic msg supplies message.
.It Cm weird
name to event_code and fallback message.
.It Cm dns
query to dns_rrname, qtype_name to dns_rrtype, rcode_name to dns_rcode,
trans_id to dns_id, and string-array answers to dns_answers.
.It Cm http
method to http_method, host to http_hostname, uri to http_url,
version to http_protocol, status_code to http_status,
user_agent to http_user_agent, username to user.
.It Cm ssl
version to tls_version, cipher to tls_cipher, server_name to tls_sni,
and Boolean resumed/established to tls_resumed/tls_established.
.It Cm files
filename to file_name, mime_type to file_mime_type, total_bytes to file_size,
seen_bytes/missing_bytes to file_seen_bytes/file_missing_bytes,
timedout to file_timedout, and md5/sha1/sha256 to
file_md5/file_sha1/file_sha256.
.El
.Pp
Counts require nonnegative 64-bit JSON integers and HTTP status requires an
integer from 100 through 599.
Each record keeps native keys, complete arrays and generic severity.
Incomplete or ambiguous shapes use generic JSON parsing.
.Ss Kubernetes audit JSON
The JSON module recognizes API server audit-log backend
.Ql audit.k8s.io/v1
.Cm Event
objects.
Each stage stays a separate record in input order.
The format remains
.Cm json ;
absent aliases default to
.Ql k8s_profile=audit/v1 ,
.Ql event_type=kubernetes_audit
and
.Ql source=kubernetes .
Existing native/generic aliases win.
.Bl -tag -width responseStatus
.It Cm auditID , stage
Supply audit_id/request_id and audit_stage.
.It Cm level
The native audit policy label becomes audit_level and original_level;
generic severity remains UNKNOWN.
.It Cm verb , requestURI , userAgent
Supply action, http_url and http_user_agent.
.It Cm user.username , user.uid
Supply actor and k8s_user_uid; native user stays a compact object string.
.It Cm impersonatedUser.username , impersonatedUser.uid
Supply target_user and k8s_impersonated_uid.
.It Cm objectRef
Its namespace, resource, name, subresource, apiGroup, apiVersion and uid supply
k8s_namespace, k8s_resource, k8s_name, k8s_subresource, k8s_api_group,
k8s_api_version and k8s_object_uid.
.It Cm responseStatus
Integer code 100 through 599 supplies http_status; string reason supplies
reason.
Explicit Success/Failure supplies outcome success/failure when the code is
absent or consistent (Success with 2xx, Failure outside 2xx).
.El
.Pp
An absent generic timestamp uses string stageTimestamp, then
requestReceivedTimestamp only if stageTimestamp is absent.
The fallback message is the literal verb and requestURI separated by a space,
or the verb alone for an empty URI.
Complete sourceIPs, groups, annotations and bodies stay native.
.Ss Security JSON profiles
The JSON module recognizes six producer exports when the record carries the
profile's trigger key; other JSON skips their predicates.
Each keeps
.Cm json ,
native keys and raw input, fills only absent aliases, and applies producer
severity only when generic JSON found none.
.Bl -tag -width journald
.It GELF 1.1
Trigger short_message; requires version "1.1" and string host.
Marker gelf_profile=1.1.
short_message supplies message; facility, _container_name or _tag supplies
source; level 0 through 7 is a syslog severity.
.It journald
.Ql journalctl -o json
output.
Trigger __REALTIME_TIMESTAMP; requires __CURSOR and _BOOT_ID.
Marker journald_profile=json.
MESSAGE, SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER, _HOSTNAME, _PID, _COMM and _CMDLINE supply message,
source, host, pid, process_name and command; PRIORITY "0" through "7" is a
syslog severity.
.It Falco
JSON alert output.
Trigger rule; requires source, hostname, time and a Falco priority name.
Marker falco_profile=alert.
output, rule and hostname supply message, signature and host; output_fields
supply user, process_name, command, pid, src_ip and dst_ip.
.It osquery
Result logs.
Trigger hostIdentifier; requires name, calendarTime, integer unixTime and one
result shape.
Marker osquery_profile=event, batch or snapshot; no severity is inferred.
.It Okta
System Log LogEvent.
Trigger eventType with a dot; requires uuid, published and object actor.
Marker okta_profile=system-log.
Aliases are event_id, event_code, timestamp, message, okta_actor, okta_result,
reason and src_ip.
.It Google Cloud Audit
LogEntry with an AuditLog protoPayload.
Trigger protoPayload.
Marker gcp_profile=audit.
Aliases are event_id, source, action, actor, src_ip, http_user_agent, outcome,
reason and a
.Ql methodName resourceName
message.
.El
.Ss Cloud and container JSON profiles
These profiles follow the security profile rules.
.Bl -tag -width CloudTrail
.It Snort
Snort 3.13.2.0 alert_json.
Trigger rule, gid or sid; requires a gid:sid:rev rule or integer gid and sid,
no event_type, a Snort timestamp or integer seconds, and a Snort action name
when action is present.
Marker snort_profile=alert_json.
Aliases are generator_id, signature_id, signature_rev, signature, category,
protocol, src_ip, dst_ip and, for TCP and UDP only, src_port and dst_port.
Priority 1 is CRITICAL, 2 ERROR and 3 WARN.
.It Docker
Docker 29 json-file driver.
Trigger log; requires only log, stream, attrs and time keys, stream stdout or
stderr, and an RFC 3339 time ending in Z.
Marker docker_profile=json-file.
log without its final newline supplies message; docker_stream and
docker_partial describe the chunk; no severity is inferred.
.It CloudTrail
CloudTrail record, eventVersion 1.08 through 1.11.
Trigger eventVersion; requires eventTime, a CloudTrail eventType, eventSource,
eventName, awsRegion, eventID, sourceIPAddress and userIdentity.type.
Marker cloudtrail_profile=event.
Aliases are event_id, timestamp, action, source, actor, user, src_ip,
aws_region, aws_account, category and http_user_agent; a nonempty errorCode
gives outcome=failure, event_code and reason.
No severity is inferred.
.It AWS WAF
AWS WAF log, formatVersion 1.
Trigger webaclId; requires integer timestamp, terminatingRuleId, a WAF
terminatingRuleType, action and httpRequest with clientIp, httpMethod, uri and
a headers array.
Marker waf_profile=aws.
Aliases are signature, waf_acl, src_ip, http_method, http_url, request_id,
country and http_status.
No severity is inferred.
.It Traefik
Traefik v3.7 JSON access log.
Trigger DownstreamStatus; requires an integer status 100 through 599,
RequestMethod and StartUTC ending in Z.
Marker traefik_profile=access.
Aliases are http_method, http_status, http_url, http_protocol, http_hostname,
router, service_name, bytes, src_ip and src_port from ClientAddr.
Status 5xx is ERROR, 4xx WARN, others INFO.
.El
.Sh SYSLOG
The
.Cm syslog
module recognizes RFC 3164, RFC 5424 and rsyslog RFC 3339 file timestamps.
It extracts timestamp, host, source, optional PID and message, plus available
priority/version/msgid/structured_data fields.
PRI severities map as follows:
.Bl -column "PRI severity" CRITICAL -offset indent
.It Sy PRI severity Ta Sy Level
.It 0, 1, 2 Ta CRITICAL
.It 3 Ta ERROR
.It 4 Ta WARN
.It 5, 6 Ta INFO
.It 7 Ta DEBUG
.El
.Pp
Without PRI, severity is UNKNOWN unless recognized authentication or Fail2ban
content supplies a severity floor.
.Ss RFC 5424 structured data
The exact section stays in
.Cm structured_data .
Decoded fields use
.Cm syslog_sd.<id>.<parameter> ;
an empty
.Cm syslog_sd.<id>
marks an element's presence.
ASCII letters, digits, underscore, hyphen and at-sign remain literal in encoded
name components; all other bytes use uppercase
.Ql %HH .
Dots and percent signs are encoded to keep names distinct.
.Pp
Names contain 1 through 32 printable ASCII bytes excluding space, equals,
closing bracket and double quote.
Value escapes decode backslash, double quote and closing bracket; unknown
escape sequences stay literal.
Repeated parameters use the last value.
The
.Cm syslog_sd_status
field is
.Cm ok , nil , invalid
or
.Cm limit .
Malformed or over-limit extraction keeps the original section with no partial
namespace aliases.
.Ss Unix authentication
Recognized English OpenSSH accepted/failed password or public-key messages and
invalid-user messages receive
.Cm event_type=authentication ,
.Cm outcome=success|failure ,
.Cm user , auth_method , src_ip
and
.Cm src_port
when present.
Addresses require literal IPv4/IPv6 and SSH ports range from 1 through 65535.
Recognized sudo command/denial and supported sshd/sudo PAM pam_unix messages
also expose role-specific actor/target_user, action and available context.
.Pp
Authentication failures have a minimum severity of ERROR; successes have a
minimum of INFO.
A higher native syslog severity is kept.
The full sudo command remains text, including embedded semicolons.
.Sh FAIL2BAN
Native records require a date/time with milliseconds, a Fail2ban logger,
bracketed PID, severity label and message.
Recognized syslog messages from fail2ban, fail2ban-server and fail2ban.* receive
the same enrichment while keeping format
.Cm syslog .
.Pp
For messages
.Ql [JAIL] Found IP ,
.Ql [JAIL] Ban IP
and
.Ql [JAIL] Unban IP ,
fields include
.Cm event_type=fail2ban , jail , src_ip
and lowercase
.Cm action=found|ban|unban .
Severity floors are WARN, ERROR and INFO respectively.
A Found message accepts a trailing producer timestamp.
Other messages keep their header severity and fields.
.Sh WEB ACCESS LOGS
The
.Cm access
module recognizes Apache/Nginx Common and Combined Log Format.
Fields include client address, user, timestamp, request, status, bytes and,
for Combined format, referer and user_agent.
The status maps 4xx to WARN, 5xx to ERROR and other statuses to INFO.
.Pp
A valid three-part HTTP request supplies method, path and protocol.
Malformed request text inside an otherwise valid access record keeps request,
message, status and severity.
Describe custom server log layouts with
.Xr lumtail-rules 5 .
.Sh WEB ERROR LOGS
The
.Cm web-error
module reads Apache HTTP Server 2.4 default file error records and Nginx 1.28
standard file error prefixes.
Both supply timestamp, source (apache or nginx), pid, optional tid, message,
level and original_level.
Timestamp text keeps its producer timezone information.
.Pp
Apache adds apache_module, optional apache_client and an initial
AH-plus-five-digit event_code.
Nginx adds optional nginx_connection and complete supported suffix fields
nginx_client, nginx_server, nginx_request, nginx_upstream, nginx_host and
nginx_referrer; request aliases nginx_request.
Unknown, repeated or malformed suffix context stays in the message without
partial context aliases.
.Pp
Validated literal clients add src_ip and optional src_port (0 through 65535).
IPv4-with-port and bracketed IPv6-with-port are supported; bare IPv6 is treated
as a whole address.
Producer trace1 through trace8 map to TRACE, debug to DEBUG, info/notice to
INFO, warn to WARN, error to ERROR and crit/alert/emerg to CRITICAL.
.Sh IDS ALERTS
The
.Cm ids.fast
module reads Snort 3 alert_fast and Suricata 7/8 fast.log lines.
Fields include timestamp, action, generator_id, signature_id, signature_rev,
signature, message, category, alert_priority, protocol, endpoints,
.Ql event_type=alert
and source (snort or suricata).
Priority 1 is CRITICAL, 2 ERROR and 3 WARN.
Suricata ICMP records publish icmp_type and icmp_code instead of ports.
.Sh AWS VPC FLOW LOGS
The
.Cm aws.vpc-flow
module reads the default 14-field version 2 record.
Fields include vpc_account_id, vpc_interface_id, endpoints, vpc_protocol,
protocol, vpc_packets, vpc_bytes, vpc_start, vpc_end, timestamp, action and
vpc_log_status.
NODATA and SKIPDATA records publish no endpoints, counters or action.
Flow records keep UNKNOWN severity.
.Sh SQUID ACCESS LOGS
The
.Cm squid.native
module reads the Squid native access.log format.
Fields include timestamp, duration_ms, src_ip, squid_result, http_status, bytes,
http_method, http_url, user, squid_hierarchy, squid_peer and http_content_type.
Status 4xx is WARN, 5xx ERROR and other statuses INFO.
A username may contain spaces.
.Sh COMMON EVENT FORMAT
The
.Cm cef
module accepts standalone CEF 0/1 or CEF at the start of a recognized RFC
3164/5424 message.
All header fields and the final pipe are required; empty values are accepted.
Header escapes decode pipe and backslash.
Extension escapes decode equals, backslash, newline and carriage return.
A space followed by
.Ql key=
starts the next extension; other spaces and literal pipes stay in values.
Duplicate keys use their last value.
.Pp
Header fields use cef_version, cef_device_vendor, cef_device_product,
cef_device_version, cef_event_class_id, cef_name and cef_severity.
Each decoded extension is available as
.Cm cef_ext_KEY ;
unprotected names are also available under their native keys.
Extensions cannot replace normalized or envelope metadata.
.Bl -tag -width suser/duser
.It Cm event class / name
Supply event_code and signature_id / signature and fallback message.
.It Cm msg
Supplies message when present, including an empty value.
.It Cm src , dst , spt , dpt
Supply literal address and decimal port aliases; ports range from 0 through
65535.
.It Cm suser , duser
Supply actor/target_user; user takes present suser, otherwise duser.
.It Cm act , cat , proto
Supply action, category and protocol.
.It Cm rt
Supplies standalone timestamp; wrapped records use their syslog timestamp.
.El
.Pp
Source is the product, then vendor, then
.Ql cef .
CEF severity controls normalized level even if the syslog envelope disagrees:
.Bl -column "CEF severity" CRITICAL -offset indent
.It Sy CEF severity Ta Sy Level
.It 0, Unknown, empty, unmapped Ta UNKNOWN
.It 1-3, Low Ta INFO
.It 4-6, Medium Ta WARN
.It 7-8, High Ta ERROR
.It 9-10, Very-High Ta CRITICAL
.El
.Pp
Text labels are case-insensitive.
Wrapped records keep available syslog fields and syslog_timestamp,
syslog_source, syslog_message and syslog_level transport aliases.
.Sh LOG EVENT EXTENDED FORMAT
The
.Cm leef
module accepts standalone LEEF 1.0/2.0 or a LEEF payload at the start of a
recognized RFC 3164/5424 message.
Vendor, product, product version and EventID must be nonempty.
EventID accepts up to 255 UTF-8 scalars.
Header pipes delimit fields and backslashes stay literal.
.Pp
LEEF 1.0 separates attributes with actual tabs.
Version 2.0 declares one delimiter scalar, either literal or lowercase
.Ql x
or
.Ql 0x
followed by one through four hexadecimal digits.
A literal pipe delimiter needs the header form
.Ql ...|EventID|||key=value|other=value .
Attribute values keep spaces, equals and backslashes.
Duplicate keys use the last value; an empty payload, empty values and one
trailing delimiter are accepted.
.Pp
Native attributes use
.Cm leef_ext_KEY .
Header fields are leef_version, leef_device_vendor, leef_device_product,
leef_device_version, leef_event_id and leef_delimiter.
The product supplies source; present msg supplies message, otherwise EventID.
EventID also supplies event_code.
.Bl -tag -width srcPort/dstPort
.It Cm usrName , cat , proto
Supply user, category and protocol.
.It Cm src , dst , srcPort , dstPort
Supply validated literal address and decimal port aliases (0 through 65535).
.It Cm devTime
Supplies timestamp when present, including an empty string; otherwise a
transport timestamp is kept.
.It Cm sev
Stays in original_level and leef_ext_sev.
Generic LEEF severity is UNKNOWN; use exact native severity filters.
.El
.Pp
Wrapped records keep envelope fields, the structured-data namespace and
available syslog transport aliases.
.Sh LINUX AUDIT RAW
The
.Cm auditd.raw
module recognizes standalone RAW records with optional node, record type and
a complete
.Ql msg=audit(SECONDS.FRACTION:SERIAL):\&
prefix.
Each physical record stays separate.
.Bl -tag -width auditd.fields.KEY.N
.It Cm auditd.type , auditd.timestamp , auditd.serial , auditd.node
Native header text; node appears only when supplied.
.It Cm timestamp , event_id , event_code , source
Epoch timestamp text, timestamp-plus-serial correlation key, native record type
and the source auditd.
.It Cm auditd.fields.KEY , auditd.msg.KEY
Last outer or compound-message value with native quotes/hex bytes kept.
.It Cm auditd.fields.KEY.N , auditd.msg.KEY.N
Every occurrence indexed from 1, including duplicates, in input order.
.It Cm pid , parent_pid , syscall
Validated unsigned decimal aliases.
.It Cm process_name , executable , path , cwd , command
Selected text aliases; command comes from USER_CMD.
.It Cm user , target_user , src_ip , action , outcome
Supported USER_AUTH/USER_LOGIN identity and result context.
.El
.Pp
Native key components keep ASCII letters, digits, underscore, hyphen and
at-sign; other bytes use uppercase percent encoding.
Only selected acct/comm/exe/name/cwd/cmd text values are unquoted or hex-decoded
for aliases.
Raw namespace values remain unchanged, and backslashes in quoted RAW text stay
literal.
.Pp
USER_AUTH/USER_LOGIN res=success maps to INFO/success; res=failed maps to
ERROR/failure.
Compound-message presence wins over outer fields, including empty or unmapped
values.
SYSCALL success supplies an outcome with UNKNOWN severity.
Other framed record types keep UNKNOWN severity and their native fields.
.Sh LOGFMT
The
.Cm logfmt.kv
module reads lines made only of two or more
.Ql key=value
pairs, following go-logfmt v0.6.0.
Keys are 1 to 128 printable ASCII bytes.
Quoted values decode JSON-style escapes.
Bare keys, unknown escapes, raw control bytes,
.Ql \eu0000 ,
.Ql msg=audit(
values and more than 1024 pairs reject the line.
A repeated key keeps its last value.
timestamp, level, source and message come from keys such as ts, time,
level, lvl, msg, logger and component.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Inspect all fields before choosing a filter:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --output json --limit 3 application.log
.Ed
.Pp
Select CEF errors or exact LEEF native severity:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --where format=cef --min-level error security.log
lumtail --where leef_ext_sev=8 --output json security.log
.Ed
.Pp
Query EVE alerts and Zeek DNS records:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --alerts-only --preset network eve.json
lumtail --where zeek_log_type=dns --output json zeek.json
.Ed
.Pp
Select Kubernetes operations by authenticated actor and audit stage:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --where actor=alice --where audit_stage=ResponseComplete \e
  --template '{action}|{k8s_resource}|{http_status}|{level}' \e
  audit.jsonl
.Ed
.Pp
Read a structured-data parameter using its exact normalized name:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --where 'syslog_sd.auth@32473.user=alice' syslog.log
.Ed
.Pp
Correlate Linux Audit records without combining them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --module auditd.raw --where auditd.serial=42 \e
  --output json audit.log
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr lumtail 1 ,
.Xr lumtail-rules 5 ,
.Xr lumtail-agent 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The parsers read records described by these specifications:
.Bl -tag -width "audit.k8s.io/v1" -offset indent
.It RFC 3164
The BSD syslog Protocol.
.It RFC 5424
The Syslog Protocol.
.It RFC 3339
Date and Time on the Internet: Timestamps, used by rsyslog file output.
.It CEF 0, 1
Common Event Format.
.It LEEF 1.0, 2.0
Log Event Extended Format.
.It audit.k8s.io/v1
Kubernetes audit Event API.
.It GELF 1.1
Graylog Extended Log Format payload.
.It go-logfmt v0.6.0
logfmt key=value records.
.It Snort 3.13.2.0
alert_json output.
.It Docker 29.x
json-file logging driver.
.It CloudTrail 1.08\(en1.11
AWS CloudTrail record contents.
.It AWS WAF 1
AWS WAF log fields, formatVersion 1.
.It Traefik v3.7
JSON access log fields.
.El
.Sh LIMITS
Unsupported or malformed input falls through to another matching parser or
plain text; line/record overflow instead produces an input error.
JSON projections require the supported producer shapes and field types.
Zeek TSV, Kubernetes EventList/webhook expansion, custom syslog layouts and
custom web-server layouts need their own rules or parser.
.Pp
The profiles describe independent observations.
EVE observations, Zeek connection states and Kubernetes response codes keep
their mapped fields without an inferred alert severity.
Kubernetes outcome requires an explicit, consistent status label.
Correlation fields such as Zeek UID/FUID and audit request IDs can span several
records.
.Pp
CEF extensions, LEEF attributes and RFC 5424 element-plus-parameter extraction
each accept at most 1024 occurrences, including duplicates.
Linux Audit accepts 256 total outer/inner occurrences, 128 bytes per native key
and one compound-message level.
Audit event assembly and syscall-name lookup need separate processing.
.Pp
The installed guide and profile designs under
.Pa share/doc/lumtail/docs/
give the producer versions and exact recognition rules.
Safety rules are in
.Pa docs/safety.md
under that documentation directory.
